1
00:05:35,294 --> 00:05:41,294
Their bodies were caged, but not their desires. They
would do anything for a man - or to him.

2
00:11:10,588 --> 00:11:16,588
The Big Doll House is a action, drama film directed by Jack Hill.

3
00:16:45,882 --> 00:16:51,882
Female prisoners in a Philippine jail are being subjected to sadistic torture.

4
00:22:21,176 --> 00:22:27,176
Five of the women -- along with the help of two men -- plot an escape.

5
00:27:56,470 --> 00:28:02,470
Collier, played by Judith Brown.

6
00:33:31,764 --> 00:33:37,764
Grear, played by Pam Grier.

7
00:39:07,058 --> 00:39:13,058
Harrad, played by Brooke Mills.

8
00:44:42,352 --> 00:44:48,352
Alcott, played by Roberta Collins.

9
00:50:17,647 --> 00:50:23,647
Bodine, played by Pat Woodell.

10
00:55:52,941 --> 00:55:58,941
Harry, played by Sid Haig.

11
01:01:28,235 --> 01:01:34,235
The Big Doll House is a 1971 American women-in-prison film starring Pam Grier,
Judy Brown, Roberta Collins, Brooke Mills, and Pat Woodell.

12
01:07:03,529 --> 01:07:09,529
The film follows six female inmates through daily life in
a gritty, unidentified tropical prison.

13
01:12:38,823 --> 01:12:44,823
Later the same year, the film Women in Cages featured a similar story and setting and
much the same cast, and was shot in the same abandoned prison buildings.

14
01:18:14,117 --> 01:18:20,117
A nonsequel follow-up, titled The Big Bird Cage, was released in 1972.

15
01:23:49,411 --> 01:23:55,411
The film explores themes of prison, rape, sadism, exploitation, women's prison.

16
01:29:24,705 --> 01:29:30,705
The tone is emotional.
